From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Mon Jun 16 16:06:20 2014
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Mon, 16 Jun 2014 16:06:20 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] how to program QTL analysis for many phenotypes on the same map
Message-ID: <oszd_7LlpOs.bUMSJXqY8YkJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <oszd_7LlpOs.biidExooULoJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <oszd_7LlpOs.biidExooULoJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/oszd_7LlpOs/m/bUMSJXqY8YkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You could use a for loop.

I wouldn't use the same object name for the genome scan and for the permutation results, as you write over the permutation results with the genome scan.

Something like this:

pheno.col <- 1:100
n.phe <- length(pheno.col)
em.cim <- hk.cim <- imp.cim <- em.cim.perm <- hk.cim.perm <- imp.cim.perm <- vector("list", n.phe)
thr.em <- thr.hk <http://thr.hk> <- thr.imp <- rep(NA, n.phe)
for(i in seq(along=pheno.col)) {
  em.cim.perm[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="em", n.perm=1000)
  thr1[i] <- summary(em.cim.perm[[i]], alpha=0.05)
  em.cim[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="em")

  hk.cim.perm[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="hk", n.perm=1000)
  thr2[i] <- summary(hk.cim.perm[[i]], alpha=0.05)
  hk.cim[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="hk")

  imp.cim.perm[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="imp",

                           map.function="kosambi", error.prob=0.0001, n.perm=1000)

  thr3[i] <- summary(imp.cim.perm[[i]], alpha=0.05)
  imp.cim[[i]] <- cim(map, pheno.col=pheno.col[i], n.marcov=5, window=10, method="imp",
                      map.function="kosambi", error.prob=0.0001)
}

karl

